### Commutant theorem

Such a joint POVM exists if and only if every `E_h` commutes with every `P_s`.

Necessity is elementary.  Positivity and `sum_h G_{h,s}=P_s` imply `0 <= G_{h,s} <= P_s`.  Because `P_s` is a projector, each `G_{h,s}` is supported in its range, so `G_{h,s}=P_sG_{h,s}=G_{h,s}P_s`.  Summing over `s` makes every `E_h` block diagonal in the sharp PVM and hence commuting.  Conversely, for commuting effects,

\[
 G_{h,s}=P_sE_h=E_hP_s
\]

is positive and has both required marginals.

Block65 menu 0 is diagonal in this basis.  It therefore has four nonzero joint branches per frame:

| outcome | `s=-` weight on `|0>` | `s=+` weight on `|1>` |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 1/2 | 0 |
| 1 | 1/2 | 1/5 |
| 2 | 0 | 4/5 |

All 24 proper frames give exact positive marginals, nonnegative Choi spectra, and the same result by co-transport.

Block65 menu 1 contains off-diagonal entries in outcomes 1 and 2.  Its commutators with the endpoint `Z` have nonzero off-diagonal magnitude `1/2` (equivalently `1/4` against each rank-one projector).  Across 24 frames the runner resolves 96 noncommuting projector/effect pairs.  Therefore menu 1 admits no joint sharp endpoint-orientation refinement.  This is a selector only between the two existing Block65 menus; it is not a no-go for every possible instrument or every unsharp current observable.

